  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will shift toward \nthe interplay between large scale troughing over the eastern \nPacific/West Coast and the subtropical high over the Southern Plains \nand Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor retrogression toward the \nDesert Southwest approaching the weekend. This evolution would not \ncompletely scour residual moisture immediately, so rain chances will \nlikely linger into Thursday. Current PoPs show 30-50% chances for \nfoothill and mountain areas and 10-30% probabilities for the South-\nCentral Arizona deserts. Beyond that, persistent southwesterly flow \nwould eventually dry us out enough to limit rain chances heading \ninto the weekend. Another influx of moisture by the end of the seven \nday period may bring rain chances back once again, but max chances \nat this time only range between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will only \nfluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the ridge \nkeeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region, as \nmentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to our \nwest will keep the high from becoming too amplified, preventing any \nfurther discussion of abnormal heat, at least during this forecast \nperiod. Temperatures for the lower deserts Thursday through Sunday \nwill range generally between 94-106 degrees, while overnight lows \nhover near 70-82 degrees.",
